#!/bin/sh
# Firewall flow offloading: ON by default (v1.3, kept in v1.4), wired IPv4
# measured >=395 Mbit/s through the PPE with the CPU at 7%.
#
# What the options do: the gmac driver has the PPE hardware path
# (zx279128s-ppe.c, TC_SETUP_FT) and the image carries kmod-nft-offload, so
# with these fw4 installs an nft flowtable; with flow_offloading_hw the flow
# block is handed to the PPE and established IPv4 TCP/UDP flows are forwarded
# without the CPU. If the PPE refuses the block, fw4 falls back to the
# software flowtable.
#
# The 2026-10-27 story, so nobody re-flips this blindly: with
# flow_offloading_hw=1 the PPE's handling of IPv6 broke IPv6 TCP completely
# (connect ok in ~1 ms, then zero payload, ICMPv6 and IPv4 unaffected) -
# measured on hardware by the zwzzrl fork. The mechanism: zx_update_traps()
# cleared the IPv6 ethertype trap whenever br-lan had members, while
# zx_update_mac_tables() had already moved our MACs out of the trap-DMAC
# table into the ONU routed table - an IPv6 frame addressed to the router was
# left with no path to the CPU. v1.4 fixes the driver instead of dropping the
# feature: zx279128s-ppe.c (overlay copy) keeps the IPv6 trap on whenever the
# PPE is active, so IPv6 always takes the software path (correct, including
# LAN-to-LAN) while IPv4 keeps the hardware path. That driver change is
# source-verified against the fork's measurement but NOT yet device-verified
# on this exact build - if IPv6 TCP stalls again, turn "Hardware flow
# offloading" off in LuCI (Network -> Firewall -> General Settings) and
# report it; that restores the known-good v1.3-off state instantly.
#
# uci set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading='1'
# uci set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading_hw='1'    # needs the first one
# uci commit firewall && fw4 restart
#
# Known limits with offloading on:
#  - IPv6 flows are never offloaded (the PPE classifier holds IPv4 rules
#    only; the driver skips them) - IPv6 always rides the CPU path;
#  - PPPoE flows may not take the hardware path (sibling ZX279133 hit exactly
#    that) - compare against a plain IP uplink if WAN is PPPoE;
#  - the PPE hash table is small (384 entries on this driver); overflow flows
#    fall back to software gracefully (driver counters: ppe_add_skip,
#    ppe_add_fail, under /sys/kernel/debug/92000000.ethernet/).
uci -q batch <<-EOF
	set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading='1'
	set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading_hw='1'
	commit firewall
EOF
exit 0
